<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listening with Attention: Entropy-Guided Explainability for Transformer-Based Audio Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></span> shows that each LEAF-X component contributes measurably: removing entropy weighting or rollout yields the largest drops in localization and sparsity (TLoc and SPR) and weaker faithfulness (higher D-AOPC and INF), while removing gradient modulation or causal reweighting primarily degrades faithfulness (D-AOPC and INF) with smaller but consistent losses in grounding and stability. Full LEAF-X achieves the best overall trade-off (lowest D-AOPC &amp; INF and highest TLoc, SPR, &amp; STAB), indicating that entropy-guided head selection, multi-layer evidence aggregation, and lightweight causal checks are complementary and jointly necessary for the most faithful, time-aligned explanations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the detailed component and hyperparameter ablations in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Component and Hyperparameter Ablations ‣ Listening with Attention: Entropy-Guided Explainability for Transformer-Based Audio Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">LEAF-X Ablation on Whisper-large-v3 (LibriSpeech).<span id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Lower is better for D-AOPC/INF; higher is better for TLoc/SPR/STAB.</span></span></figcaption>
